===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|informal}} One who is [[pompous]] or [[self-important]], especially one who is [[officious]] in a position of authority.
#* {{quote-book|en|book|year=1914|author=Samuel Hopkins Adams|authorlink=Samuel Hopkins Adams|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/16447/16447-h/16447-h.htm|title=The Clarion|chapter=30|passage="Don't you come the high-and-holy on me. You and your smooth, big, phony '''stuffed-shirt''' of a father."}}
#* {{quote-av|en|title={{w|Citizen Kane}}|author=Herman J. Mankiewicz; Orson Welles|year=1941|medium=motion picture|publisher=RKO Radio Pictures|actor=Joseph Cotten|role=Jedediah Leland|passage=Bernstein, am I a '''stuffed shirt'''? Am I a horse-faced hypocrite? Am I a New England school marm?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=26 May 1952|url=http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,859638,00.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20121118201431/https://time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,859638,00.html|archivedate=2012-12-01|title=Medicine: Mind Matters|magazine=Time|passage=Dr. Laughlin was the only one in a movie party who detested the second male lead—"I regarded him as overserious, pedantic, a '''stuffed shirt'''."}}
# {{lb|en|usually|hyphenated}} Used [[attributive]]ly.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=10 July 2000|author=Frederik Balfour|url=http://www.businessweek.com/archives/2000/b3689207.arc.htm|title=Return of a Hong Kong Highflier|work=BusinessWeek|passage=The opportunistic style that was the bank's trademark still prevails and continues to attract talented young Hong Kongers looking for an alternative to the '''stuffed-shirt''' culture of most U.S. and European banks.}}

